From 6abdd48551248d5a46fdc6f91e64c5243a650ecd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 20:06:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make likeness attestation universal across providers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The likeness-rights record was welded to one lane: uploaded sources only,
atlascloud only, Wan reference-to-video models only. Everything else in a
13-image / 7-video provider catalog was silent, AI-generated photorealistic
characters carried no record at all, and the BytePlus asset lane — the one
being pursued for Seedance asset ids — had no KOKAO-side rights record,
deciding purely on whether a provider identity row said "verified".

Worse, likenessPolicyVersion() hashed the selected image-processor scope into
the policy version, so an admin changing the global image provider marked
every attestation in the system stale: a statement about who is in a
photograph invalidated by routing that does not change who is in it.

Split the record in two. The grant covers the SUBJECT — adult, image rights,
self vs authorized person, and three separable uses — and is provider
independent. An append-only recipient ledger covers WHO RECEIVES them, so a
newly configured provider costs one acknowledgement instead of a
re-signature, and one recipient can be withdrawn without destroying an
attestation that remains true. Provider-independent is not use-independent:
permission to depict someone is still not permission to put words in their
mouth.

Add generated_fictional as a third subject class, covered by a workspace
standing declaration rather than a per-character signature because the server
creates generated cast with no user in the loop. That declaration is also the
evidence that a photorealistic AI face depicts nobody real — the argument
against the classifier false positives that have been blocking this path.

Add a reviewed per-provider eligibility table with two independent axes, since
a classifier that refuses a real face usually refuses a generated one too.
undeclared fails closed and exhaustiveness is asserted against both registries,
so a provider cannot be added and silently start receiving faces. Routing is
checked before the attestation and before funding, so a provider that will
refuse the image fails fast with a reason instead of taking payment first.

Collect the attestation in the same transaction as the character row. It was
previously a separate call behind a panel mounted in two screens, so a
character could be created, sheeted and dressed — spending image credits at
each step — and only fail at video funding.

Job snapshots become a version union: existing v1 rows keep rendering under
exactly the authorization they were funded against, and v2 binds a job to the
disclosure it was funded against as well as the grant.

